# Netlify/Cloudflare-style host config. Vercel does NOT read this file — vercel.json
# is the live configuration — but it ships in the build, so it must not contradict it.
#
# It used to be a single `/*  /index.html  200`, which is the exact catch-all that was
# removed from vercel.json: it cannot tell a client route from a missing file, so a
# deploy that shipped without its JS answers /assets/index-a1b2.js with 200 and the
# HTML shell, and the browser fails on "Unexpected token '<'".
#
# The asset rule carries no `!` force flag, so it never shadows a file that exists —
# it only fires on a miss, which is precisely when a 404 is the honest answer.
/assets/*    /assets/:splat   404
/*           /index.html      200
